{"results":[{"url":"https://therealmediang.com/news/nigeria-at-independence-what-did-the-new-nation-look-like","final_url":"https://therealmediang.com/news/nigeria-at-independence-what-did-the-new-nation-look-like","title":"Nigeria at Independence: What Did the New Nation Look Like?","description":"When Nigeria became independent on October 1, 1960, the country that inherited sovereignty from Britain…","language":"en","text":"When Nigeria became independent on October 1, 1960, the country that inherited sovereignty from Britain was very different from the Nigeria we know today.\n\nThere were no 36 states, no Abuja, no naira, no mobile phones and no internet. Oil had been discovered, but Nigeria was not yet an oil-dependent economy. Most Nigerians lived in rural communities, agriculture dominated the economy, and modern infrastructure was concentrated in a relatively small number of cities.\n\nYet this was not a country starting from nothing.\n\nNigeria had universities, newspapers, radio, television, banks, railways, ports, factories, hospitals, a growing professional class and a vibrant cultural scene.\n\nSo, what did the new nation actually look like?\n\nNigeria had a population of roughly 36 to 45 million people around independence, depending on the demographic estimate used. The uncertainty reflected the difficulties of conducting reliable population counts during the colonial period.\n\nWhat is clearer is where most Nigerians lived.\n\nThe country was overwhelmingly rural. Historical United Nations-based estimates put the urban population at only about 15 per cent in 1960.\n\nFor most Nigerians, life revolved around farming, markets, villages, extended families, religious institutions and local communities. Cities were growing, but they were still small compared with the sprawling urban centres Nigerians know today.\n\nThe political map was also dramatically different.\n\nAt independence, Nigeria consisted principally of three large regions — Northern, Western and Eastern Nigeria — alongside the Federal Territory of Lagos.\n\nThe Northern Region was the largest geographically and had the largest population. The Western Region was centred largely around the Yoruba-speaking southwest, while the Eastern Region contained a large Igbo population alongside numerous other communities.\n\nThese regions were not politically or culturally identical, and regional competition was already a defining feature of Nigerian politics.\n\nThe Mid-Western Region did not exist yet; it was carved out of the Western Region in 1963.\n\nThere was also no Abuja. Lagos was the federal capital and the centre of national political and commercial life.\n\nLagos was already Nigeria’s principal political and commercial city, housing the Federal Government, Parliament, major businesses, the country’s leading port and much of its media activity.\n\nBut it was not the giant metropolis it is today.\n\nIbadan was a major political, commercial and educational centre in the West. Kano dominated commercial activity in the North and was closely linked to the region’s famous groundnut trade. Kaduna served as the administrative centre of Northern Nigeria, while Enugu was an important political and industrial city in the East.\n\nPort Harcourt was emerging as a major commercial and port city, particularly as petroleum production began to develop.\n\nCities such as Benin, Onitsha, Aba, Jos, Zaria, Calabar, Abeokuta, Sokoto and Maiduguri were also important regional centres.\n\nBut beyond these urban hubs was a much larger rural Nigeria.\n\nNigeria at independence was fundamentally an agricultural economy.\n\nCocoa, groundnuts, palm oil, palm kernels, cotton, rubber, timber, hides and skins were among the country’s major commodities.\n\nDifferent regions had different economic strengths. Cocoa was strongly associated with the Western Region. Groundnuts and cotton were major Northern products, while palm products were particularly important in the East and parts of the South.\n\nAgriculture provided employment, food and much of the foreign exchange needed to pay for imports.\n\nThe famous groundnut pyramids of Kano symbolised the scale of agricultural commerce in the North, while cocoa production helped make the Western Region one of the country’s major export economies.\n\nNigeria was therefore already connected to international markets — but largely as an exporter of agricultural and mineral commodities and an importer of manufactured goods.\n\nOne of the biggest misconceptions about Nigeria in 1960 is imagining that the country was already the oil giant it would become.\n\nIt was not.\n\nCommercial oil was discovered at Oloibiri in the Niger Delta in 1956, and Nigeria began exporting crude oil in 1958.\n\nBy independence, petroleum had become an emerging industry, but it remained relatively small compared with agriculture.\n\nThe enormous transformation of Nigeria’s economy around petroleum would come later, particularly after the expansion of production in the 1960s and the oil boom of the 1970s.\n\nIn 1960, Nigeria was still primarily an agricultural economy with an oil industry waiting to become much more important.\n\nNigeria also inherited important mineral resources.\n\nTin mining on the Jos Plateau was a significant export industry, while coal mining around Enugu supplied energy for industry and railway operations.\n\nManufacturing was developing too, although on a modest scale.\n\nTextiles, tobacco, cement, brewing, food processing, soap and vegetable-oil production were among the industries emerging in the late colonial period.\n\nThe development of Kaduna’s textile industry and cement production reflected an early attempt to reduce dependence on imported manufactured goods.\n\nBut Nigeria was not yet an industrial economy. Most manufactured products, machinery and consumer goods still came from abroad.\n\nEducation was one of the clearest signs of Nigeria’s transformation before independence.\n\nPrimary-school enrolment was expanding rapidly, but access varied dramatically between regions.\n\nThe Western Region had introduced free primary education in 1955, producing a major expansion in school enrolment. The South generally had a much larger educated population than the North by independence.\n\nSecondary education remained limited, while higher education was still an elite pursuit.\n\nNigeria had only a small pool of university students when it became independent. University College Ibadan, established in 1948, was the country’s first university-level institution.\n\nThe University of Nigeria, Nsukka, opened in 1960, becoming the country’s first full-fledged indigenous university.\n\nThe new nation therefore inherited an education system that was expanding rapidly but remained deeply unequal.\n\nNigeria had hospitals and medical facilities at independence, but healthcare was far from universal.\n\nModern medical services were concentrated in major towns and administrative centres, leaving many rural communities with limited access to formal healthcare.\n\nInfectious diseases such as malaria, tuberculosis, measles, smallpox and meningitis remained significant public-health concerns.\n\nElectricity was similarly uneven.\n\nPower generation existed in cities and industrial centres, but electricity was not a normal feature of everyday life for the majority of Nigerians. Rural electrification was extremely limited.\n\nFor many families, appliances that are considered basic today — refrigerators, electric cookers, fans and televisions — were luxuries or simply unavailable.\n\nThe railway was one of the most important pieces of infrastructure Nigeria inherited.\n\nRail lines connected agricultural and mining areas to commercial centres and ports, helping move groundnuts, tin, coal and agricultural products to the coast.\n\nRoad transportation was expanding, but many roads remained relatively basic. Outside the major towns, walking, bicycles, motorcycles and other forms of local transport remained common.\n\nThe country’s ports were essential to its economy. Lagos was the leading gateway for international trade, while Port Harcourt was particularly important to the Eastern Region and the emerging petroleum industry.\n\nAir travel existed, with Lagos and Kano serving as important aviation centres, but flying was largely restricted to officials, businesspeople and wealthier travellers.\n\nFor the majority, road and rail remained the practical means of long-distance travel.\n\nDespite its limited infrastructure, Nigeria already had a lively media environment.\n\nNewspapers were influential in political and social life. Publications such as the West African Pilot, Daily Times, Nigerian Tribune, Nigerian Citizen and Morning Post helped shape public debate.\n\nRadio was even more important because it reached people regardless of literacy.\n\nAnd Nigeria was already a television pioneer.\n\nWestern Nigeria Television began broadcasting from Ibadan in 1959, becoming the first television service in Africa south of the Sahara.\n\nTelevision sets, however, were expensive and uncommon. Public viewing centres allowed communities to watch programmes together.\n\nLiterature was also flourishing. Chinua Achebe’s Things Fall Apart had appeared in 1958, while writers such as Wole Soyinka, Cyprian Ekwensi and Gabriel Okara were contributing to an emerging Nigerian literary identity.\n\nThe Nigeria of 1960 was not defined only by politics and economics.\n\nHighlife was one of the dominant popular musical forms, with bands performing in hotels, clubs and social gatherings across the country. Musicians such as Victor Olaiya, Rex Lawson and Bobby Benson became prominent names.\n\nFootball was already developing into a national passion and provided one of the few popular spaces where Nigerians from different regions could rally behind a common national team.\n\nFashion also reflected the meeting of tradition and modernity. Nigerians wore Western-style clothing alongside traditional fabrics and garments such as adire, aso-oke and akwete.\n\nIn literature, music, sport and fashion, Nigerians were already negotiating what it meant to be modern while retaining distinct cultural identities.\n\nThe Nigeria That Became Independent\n\nNigeria entered independence with a mixture of promise and limitations.\n\nIt had a large population, but most people lived in rural communities. It had a productive agricultural economy, but depended heavily on imported manufactured goods. It had an emerging oil industry, but agriculture remained the economic powerhouse.\n\nIt had universities, schools and a growing professional class, but educational access was deeply uneven. It had newspapers, radio and pioneering television, but telecommunications remained extremely limited.\n\nIt had railways, roads and ports, but infrastructure was concentrated around major economic centres.\n\nAnd politically, it had inherited a federal system dominated by three powerful regions, each with different political traditions and development experiences.\n\nThe Nigeria of 1960 was therefore neither the undeveloped blank slate sometimes imagined nor the modern state that exists today.\n\nIt was a young country in transition — predominantly rural but increasingly urban, agriculturally powerful but beginning to industrialise, culturally vibrant and politically ambitious.\n\nMost importantly, independence handed Nigerians responsibility for deciding what to do with the institutions, resources and contradictions they had inherited.\n\nThe green-white-green flag represented sovereignty.\n\nThe harder task was building a nation around it.\n\nTop stories in your inbox every morning. No spam.\n\nBy subscribing, you agree to receive the Real Daily Brief and accept our Privacy Policy.","author":"David Egbede","published_date":"2026-10-01T14:00:00.000Z","latency_ms":482.7530869806651,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}